<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashanti Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Asante Empire, Asante, Ashanti, Asante Kingdom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ashanti Empire was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> state in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a> around the end of the seventeenth century with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>. Its power rested on gold, trade, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, and a strong army, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a> symbolised its unity. After a series of wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> it was annexed in 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire.jpg" alt="Ashanti Empire" width="320" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asante_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Asante Empire, Asanteman, also called the Ashanti Empire, was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> state in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>. The name Asante means because of war, from the Twi ɔsa, war, and nti, because of, and comes from the state's origin as a kingdom created to fight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a>; Ashanti is the British transcription. The ancestors of the Asante moved north from Amansie, remembered by the Akan as their early homeland, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> area as the Akan heartland broke up in disputes over succession and trade. Denkyira, which controlled much of the region's gold, made Oti Akenten, ruler of Kwaman, pay annual tribute and send a royal relative to its court, and he sent his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a>.</p><p>Around 1680 leadership of the Asante union passed to Obiri Yeboa, whose death in battle with the Dormaa led to the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a>. When the Denkyirahene Ntim Gyakari demanded heavy tribute and the Asante refused, war broke out around 1698. In tradition the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>, Sika dwa, became the central symbol of unity under Osei Tutu, with his adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okomfo-anokye">Okomfo Anokye</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Asante conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a> in 1701 they were the dominant inland power, with access to the coast and its trade with Europeans, and inland trade shifted toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>. The economy rested on gold and agricultural exports, slave trading, crafts and trade with markets further north. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a> was killed in 1717 in a war with Akyem, and the Aowin sacked Kumasi. In 1721 and 1722 the Asante defeated Aowin and Nzima, and Aowin prisoners were sold as slaves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast">Cape Coast</a> and Anomabo. By 1816, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-bonsu">Osei Bonsu</a>, the Asante had absorbed the Fante Confederacy.</p><p>The Asante held their own against the British in the first two of the five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-ashanti-wars">Anglo-Ashanti Wars</a>, killing General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maccarthy">Sir Charles MacCarthy</a> in 1824, but the British later burnt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-golden-stool">War of the Golden Stool</a>, from March to September 1900, the court members not exiled, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaa-asantewaa">Yaa Asantewaa</a>, Queen-Mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ejisu">Ejisu</a>, attacked the British Residents at Kumasi Fort. The British won and exiled her and others to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>, where King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a> already was.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Asante state was centralised. The family, under its Abusua Panyin, was the basic political unit, and villages under an Odikro were grouped into divisions under an Ohene, which formed states under an Omanhene, and all the states formed the empire under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a>. The Asante Kingdom survives today as a constitutionally protected traditional state within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Asante Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early nineteenth century <b>Asante</b>, the most powerful state of the Akan interior, pressed southward to protect its trade: it invaded the coast in 1807, fought the Ga-Fante War of 1811 and the Ashanti–Akim–Akwapim War of 1814–16, and in 1817 the African Company of Merchants recognised its…</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…linked the caliphate to Tripoli and other North African termini across the desert, westward to the kola-producing forests of <b>Asante</b>, southward to the Yoruba markets, and eastward to Bornu and the Lake Chad basin, with cloth, leather goods, kola nuts and slaves among the principal commodities of exchange.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Asante</b>, an Akan people, ranked their people by lineage, by office and by freedom.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ashanti">Social Hierarchy of the Ashanti Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Akyem Abuakwa allied with the British against <b>Ashanti</b> and came under British rule with the Gold Coast Colony in 1874.</blockquote><a href="/state/akyem-abuakwa">Akyem Abuakwa</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1900 the governor's demand for the Golden Stool, the sacred symbol of the <b>Asante</b> nation, provoked a rising led by Yaa Asantewaa, queen mother of Ejisu.</blockquote><a href="/state/ashanti-colony">Ashanti Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>Its people and royal stools were absorbed into the <b>Ashanti</b> system, and the Techiman traditional state claims succession from Bono.</blockquote><a href="/state/bono-state">Bono State</a></li></ul></section>
